1. THE LMNO FRAMEWORK

Mathematics is expressed through four irreducible functions:

L — Structure (构)
M — Interpretation (释)
N — Regime (度)
O — Realisation (行)


⸻

1.1 Structure (L)

Defines:
	•	abstract objects
	•	relations
	•	formal systems

Examples:
	•	numbers
	•	sets
	•	functions
	•	equations

⸻

1.2 Interpretation (M)

Defines:
	•	meaning
	•	mental construction
	•	conceptual understanding

Examples:
	•	intuition of number
	•	geometric imagination
	•	symbolic comprehension

⸻

1.3 Regime (N)

Defines:
	•	rules of validity
	•	proof systems
	•	logical consistency

Examples:
	•	axioms
	•	inference rules
	•	proof frameworks

⸻

1.4 Realisation (O)

Defines:
	•	execution
	•	calculation
	•	application

Examples:
	•	solving equations
	•	computation
	•	algorithmic processes

⸻

2. MATHEMATICS AS A SYSTEM

Mathematics emerges as:

Structure → Interpretation → Regime → Realisation → Continuation

Where:
	•	structure provides form
	•	interpretation provides meaning
	•	regime provides admissibility
	•	realisation provides execution
	•	continuation ensures persistence across time and agents

⸻

3. REINTERPRETING PHILOSOPHICAL SCHOOLS

3.1 Logicism → Structure (L)

Claim:

Mathematics reduces to logic.

Interpretation:
	•	privileges structural form
	•	ignores interpretation and execution

⸻

3.2 Formalism → Regime (N)

Claim:

Mathematics is rule-based symbol manipulation.

Interpretation:
	•	privileges consistency
	•	abstracts away meaning

⸻

3.3 Intuitionism → Interpretation (M)

Claim:

Mathematics is mental construction.

Interpretation:
	•	privileges cognition
	•	rejects non-constructible objects

⸻

3.4 Platonism → Structure (L + Identity emphasis)

Claim:

Mathematical objects exist independently.

Interpretation:
	•	treats structure as ontologically real
	•	emphasises discovery over construction

⸻

4. SYNTHESIS

These schools are not contradictory.

They are:

pillar-dominant projections of the same system

