5. FAILURE OF REDUCTION

Attempts to reduce mathematics to a single pillar fail because:
	•	L without M → meaningless symbols
	•	M without N → inconsistent reasoning
	•	N without L → rule without object
	•	O without L/M/N → blind execution

⸻

6. COMPLETENESS CONDITION

A mathematical system is complete when:

L + M + N + O are present and aligned

And:
	•	structure is well-defined
	•	meaning is coherent
	•	rules are consistent
	•	execution is possible

⸻

7. CONTINUATION

Mathematics persists because:
	•	structures are transmissible
	•	interpretations are shareable
	•	regimes are enforceable
	•	realisations are repeatable

Thus:

Mathematics is a continuation system

